[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\ &= {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064069 :question/id #uuid "e0bb9783-9d30-5139-a2c0-aae55ea18733" :question/math-academy-id "e-410" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{1}^2\\dfrac 1 {x^3}\\, \\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{-2}-{1}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064075 :question/id #uuid "e416cb5c-22df-55e4-a3cb-c6b5dfc650c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-448" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int\\sqrt{1-5x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064080 :question/id #uuid "109a6d6e-8e62-5eac-bfa8-7c059399efe8" :question/math-academy-id "e-457" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2}x(1-2x)^5\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1-2x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x = \\dfrac 1 2 (1-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x(1-2x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}(1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}({u}^{5}-{u}^{6})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{7}{u}^{7})|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}([0-0]-[\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{1}{42}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{168}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064094 :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064098 :question/id #uuid "cf016fa7-c3a2-5f9b-84a6-7b005d0d7286" :question/math-academy-id "e-487" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099 :question/id #uuid "60a223c7-3d1c-50d6-be7c-330ba039635c" :question/math-academy-id "e-491" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064102 :question/id #uuid "6e148f32-5352-5368-a08a-6da278a6068d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5124" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} = ay + b,$  what are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= \\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2y - \\dfrac 1 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(2)}_{a}y+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064103 :question/id #uuid "41294f0b-dc6a-5a84-a7f8-7c2370b5733c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5125" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} = p + qa + rb,$ what are the values of $p,$ $q,$ and $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}_{p}+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}_{q}a+\\underbrace{(4)}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064105 :question/id #uuid "2e0348cd-6ee1-5e95-8164-2edc58084312" :question/math-academy-id "e-558" :question/problem "Find the total finite area enclosed between the curves $y=f(x)=x^3+x^2+1$ and $y=g(x)=x^2+x+1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9eb2dc598188e6e9cac3a459fa41de9d2bba246e10af798fee3459d466b7f5f1.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} + x^{2} + 1 &= x^{2} + x + 1 \\\\\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/03/03ec1d82eb7375ce3f39a6e55d2271125d55db3fe957ac2c6a97f4dc6754de8d.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-1,0]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$A_{1}|= \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}(f(x) - g(x))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}((x^{3} + x^{2} + 1) - (x^{2} + x + 1))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx; = \\left.(\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{-1}^{0}; = 0 - (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}); = 0 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right); = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $x \\in [0,1]$, and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$A_{2}|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(g(x) - f(x))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}((x^{2} + x + 1) - (x^{3} + x^{2} + 1))dx; =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - x)dx; = \\left.- (\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{0}^{1}; = - (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}) + 0; = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117 :question/id #uuid "aaeb0407-771b-5dbc-8a50-e7bc0e3477c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6209" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064128 :question/id #uuid "ceddd310-5fe4-5b55-8210-991ff22c66b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7349" :question/problem "Peter is standing on the bank of a river. He observes a speed boat $B$ going downstream at a constant distance of $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the bank. If the boat moves at a constant speed of $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$ parallel to the bank, at what rate is the angle $\\theta,$ between the observer's line of sight to the boat and the bank, decreasing when $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6?$ Assume the angle of elevation is decreasing at a **positive** rate, and do not round the answer.\n\n![](images/ab/abf063d149ae14c11b9ca62208a4390b32ae191418c2e0fe77f6038479fd5625.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064133 :question/id #uuid "5a66344e-2092-5bd6-a4d0-5663f83866b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7556" :question/problem "A conical tank contains water. The height of the tank is $3\\,\\text{m}$ and the radius at the top is $4\\,\\text{m}.$ If water is leaking from the bottom of the tank at a rate of $6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min}$, how fast is the water level $h$ decreasing when $h=2\\,\\text{m}?$\n\n![](images/6d/6de28f823109694d40bfb02afef571c2b0d497b92efbbc738ccfa15dc7341234.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. We call $H=3\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the tank, and $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4h}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {\\left(\\frac{4h}{3}\\right)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi ({2}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{64}{9}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{27}{32}\\,\\text{m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of  $\\dfrac{27}{32}\\, \\text{m/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064147 :question/id #uuid "7db11c8c-344b-5d87-a8ae-3b006e5bd92b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9846" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{g(x)(x+3)^2}{(x+5)^7}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+5)^{6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}\\left(\\frac{3(x+5)-6(x+3)}{(x+3)(x+5)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}\\left(\\frac{-3x-3}{(x+3)(x+5)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3(x+1)(x+3)^{2}}{(x+5)^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{3(x+1)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064148 :question/id #uuid "e0cec84d-96cf-5fed-ba05-a5cfc57841a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9847" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\ny' =  \\dfrac{g(x)}{3\\sqrt[3]{x(x^5+1)^4}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2/3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}+1}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}\\left(\\frac{2({x}^{5}+1)-5{x}^{5}}{x({x}^{5}+1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}\\left(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{x({x}^{5}+1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}\\left(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}({x}^{5}+1)^{3}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x({x}^{5}+1)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-3x^5+2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2295f789-3426-5c13-8072-48ad046a0397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250848} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4cefaf-cc26-5de6-9514-472253277759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250939} {:answer/id #uuid "7672b2ad-aa8f-5d9b-b569-2cc387ccbabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251527} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2f7df9-5402-574e-adb2-2561ceb6b638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252072} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e85065-6ff4-5195-b17d-22aae81082ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2295f789-3426-5c13-8072-48ad046a0397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d653fa-fe23-5a9d-871d-2b6ab43cc543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff0caf62-efde-5e81-b5d3-dad00511c8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-12019" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb0d9b8c-6095-5d0b-99f7-208fc4411b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{9q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061196} {:answer/id #uuid "1b81a56c-810f-573a-97dd-301514efe747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9|p|}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481592} {:answer/id #uuid "9b93bd3e-5333-5ae7-b2a4-a70e11e54551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{p}{9q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481615} {:answer/id #uuid "a58d28b3-647f-5745-b92e-54ec7959c4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|p|}{9|q|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481618} {:answer/id #uuid "eade159a-6f25-56c3-9356-c512910ef15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9p}{|q|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58d28b3-647f-5745-b92e-54ec7959c4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|p|}{9|q|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481618} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa61df20-116f-58fd-ad91-75f98fec1c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dae30c03-3248-5393-af33-6d1d5e3a7a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-129392" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{81{q}^{2}}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${p}^{2},81,$ and ${q}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{81{q}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{{9}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot 9\\cdot q\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot q\\cdot 9\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p}{9q}\\cdot \\frac{p}{9q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{p}{9q}\\right)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{p}{9q}\\right)}^{2}} & =|\\frac{p}{9q}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9|\\cdot |q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{9|q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e0f34b-5097-5a20-adb3-842ace671abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273008} {:answer/id #uuid "7197ad91-2e27-5302-a0bb-198625176908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273360} {:answer/id #uuid "78d4fcca-2528-5a82-a9af-92ef042fe263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273421} {:answer/id #uuid "a792fc35-f0c2-5558-95be-16d569845362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273837} {:answer/id #uuid "d1654612-6bd8-5d58-aae6-54bb0834b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48e0f34b-5097-5a20-adb3-842ace671abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273008} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f653706-bdb6-5ba7-8a0c-b039d59d4e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acbc28aa-c46b-5e1e-aadd-c2c3d31e5dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-155515" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d80feb-f2c6-5f50-998d-140615bb423f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273073} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e2ed0e-9584-529e-a252-b34f358a9fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273831} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a29cb3-e149-5839-9e18-5e1629b1597b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274111} {:answer/id #uuid "cab61c42-d6bd-5a32-9ca0-1c64a3c9a198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274150} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ae742c-09f3-5acc-ab56-490ba51b52e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab61c42-d6bd-5a32-9ca0-1c64a3c9a198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274150} :answer-field/id #uuid "d016aeae-b329-51bf-bd8b-1719296023cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e3de030-cb78-5dd0-88f5-7229520c5041" :question/math-academy-id "q-15667" :question/problem "![](images/24/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400b63a0-4683-5a21-897e-9f816d13ecf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285387} {:answer/id #uuid "72dc0745-c711-5555-b758-8857ea08db0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285845} {:answer/id #uuid "7e97b556-fb4d-50ab-8db3-611fbf05e7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285953} {:answer/id #uuid "85326801-a074-5ff0-bebe-99064aa9feb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286012} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f53bfe-9dac-5f4c-b151-f462bed9f133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72dc0745-c711-5555-b758-8857ea08db0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285845} :answer-field/id #uuid "a839d901-bb07-5422-9156-0f0bc9d6308d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa336cf9-b3b8-5d4a-a543-f9cee172da34" :question/math-academy-id "q-19394" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b745eac-288c-519f-827e-2937ae90553b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061615} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a41a38-3f10-5342-9dc5-04a2ecfcc001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061616} {:answer/id #uuid "3df2c088-75da-58f8-942c-5d4b3a14d502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061617} {:answer/id #uuid "419036f6-f9dd-5b25-a7a0-84c4a9c291ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061618} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6555da-0526-5464-be80-e4fe3bd7facd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df2c088-75da-58f8-942c-5d4b3a14d502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061617} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6c3185-c6b2-5724-9230-4a66915228ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2435a5d9-0223-53db-acdd-e3c8030d82a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-20948" :question/problem "Find  the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/ae/aef1d6e33213def71ada4a59f7d79b9f045083b2c501a752e81994e33f100277.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f9fb81-1660-5d2f-8367-cb9e54b394f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481144} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2673aa-48dc-51fb-bef1-5108cb413aa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b6f2199-f93d-54e4-a4b1-9693a8b2262c" :question/math-academy-id "q-217025" :question/problem "$2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3})=${{field-1}}\n$\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${b}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) &= 2{b}^{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{b}{4}\\right)+2{b}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}+2{b}^{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)({b}^{3+1})+2({b}^{3+3})+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right){b}^{3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}+2{b}^{6}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3} \\\\ &= 2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6944127-6661-5b25-9de1-55457d5eb6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061720} {:answer/id #uuid "e651bfa8-7deb-5973-bd08-f185ede17cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061721} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7f2ce7-2a7d-5654-8960-0d71acdc5032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061722} {:answer/id #uuid "2f80da8f-3391-54d0-81bf-d757832bfbf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061723} {:answer/id #uuid "67767361-f579-5fb8-bae5-fdc9772fd581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061724}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9d473d8e-f0b0-551a-9f97-35f1959777b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061719}] :question/id #uuid "541f1855-c508-5f2a-b424-8fac5de18666" :question/math-academy-id "q-2213" :question/problem "What is $(\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right))$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #u