17592186393177} {:answer/id #uuid "dafb3fc0-f6a1-5617-964a-36080cd6f04c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393178} {:answer/id #uuid "577baa3c-ff60-5e2e-9451-c051fd6e20b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba94734-6539-5f8b-b9fa-953023f4379c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393176} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bc4a65-3fa0-525a-8a5b-ebdeb649b1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c20eb755-854c-51a7-aed2-16e438744a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-7175" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{5t-10}{t},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{5t-10}{t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(2)=\\frac{10}{(2)^{2}}=\\frac{10}{4}=2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|2.5|=2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3cb6f9-311e-5c6f-968f-d329ca876acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523349} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523384} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb8f76-f47c-5af3-a659-05c32947975b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523610} {:answer/id #uuid "d59b9e51-13c3-51f1-9ffd-54c886c3a3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "396\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523703} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e695d2-b727-526d-9159-909e8a375b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523384} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0f255b-23e8-5379-8b30-b0353a515cfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "438beae6-a258-51c5-a564-575bb144d947" :question/math-academy-id "q-85823" :question/problem "The radius of the base of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h}.$ The height of the cone is always half its radius. At what rate is the cone's volume decreasing when the radius of its base is $12\\,\\text{m}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{r}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :question/id #uuid "5ab90279-394b-5df0-8bf3-a1042196b40c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1228" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y = k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf9fe67940a4257bad36511847e5cdb91974993e3be6d113dbc62dadd782134.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5$, hence $c=-5.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-5 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}-5 & =-1 \\\\ k-5 & =-1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $y = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} - 5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068468 :question/id #uuid "c52719a7-544b-5844-b716-9b9d2999c028" :question/math-academy-id "e-1358" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{a^3}{(b-1)^3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{a}^{3}}{(b-1)^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{a}{b-1}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{a}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068494 :question/id #uuid "094b2695-32c7-55dd-b3e1-27afcd3a09c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14229" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{2}z-2| & =3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}z-2 & =3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}z & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\dfrac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}z & =5 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2}z) & =\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (5) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{2}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{7}{2}}z) & =\\frac{10}{7} \\\\ z & =\\frac{10}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{7}{2}z-2\\right),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{2}z-2| & =3 \\\\ -(\\frac{7}{2}z-2) & =3 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}z+2 & =3 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\dfrac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2}z & =1 \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2}z) & =\\left(-\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (1) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{2}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{7}{2}}z) & =-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z = -\\dfrac{2}{7}$ and $z = \\dfrac{10}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068504 :question/id #uuid "648e9e50-03bf-56ba-a4ad-caf142c9628a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14574" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-2x + 3\n& = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\\\[3pt]\n& = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\\\[3pt]\n& = (x-1)^2+2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n=\n\\arctan u + C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068507 :question/id #uuid "4b6820ef-32a8-5d26-84f6-a464db90129a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14577" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{36 -(3x - 2)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36(1-(3x-2)^{2}/36)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x-2}{6}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068563 :question/id #uuid "fd2d4aca-7859-5bed-a514-43d82ba25400" :question/math-academy-id "e-4102" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+4.$ Then $2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral,  we use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068572 :question/id #uuid "b81ff533-2067-5d1a-b265-df1ad91fa8da" :question/math-academy-id "e-496" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^e 32x^3(\\ln x)^2 \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068579 :question/id #uuid "e90d590d-956d-5bb7-af6c-7572051b7f1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4969" :question/problem "Factor $1 - \\dfrac 1 {t^9}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{1}{t^9}  = 1^3 - \\left( \\dfrac{1}{t^3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{9}} & ={1}^{3}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068580 :question/id #uuid "5b83575b-9674-5dc5-b3eb-a918f145be93" :question/math-academy-id "e-497" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2{x}}{x} \\textrm{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068586 :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3 & =A(2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)+B(2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3 & =A(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)+B(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068587 :question/id #uuid "2fb19e4b-c455-599f-8856-5bee85f8efc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-508" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n2x+3 = A(x+1)+B .\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+3 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ 1 & =0+B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 3 & =A+B \\\\ 3 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2x+3}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1]-[\\frac{1}{2}-1] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068588 :question/id #uuid "789c5233-a263-55a7-9b27-c834b2ceaf8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-509" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3}\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{A}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x-1)^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2x-1 & =A(3x-1)^{2}+B(3x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-1 & =A{(3\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-1)}^{2}+B(3\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-1)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(0)}^{2}+2(0)-1 & =A{(0-1)}^{2}+B(0-1)+C \\\\ -1 & =A-B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-B & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get another equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(1)}^{2}+2(1)-1 & =A{(3(1)-1)}^{2}+B(3(1)-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A+2B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2A+B & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding our two equations $A-B=-\\dfrac{5}{3}$ and $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ 3A & =3 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+B & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ B & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3} = \\dfrac{1}{(3x-1)} +\\dfrac{8}{3(3x-1)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{3(3x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{8}{3(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{3(3x-1)^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x-1}\\text{d}x+\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|+\\frac{8}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3(3x-1)})+\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{6(3x-1)^{2}})+K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|-\\frac{8}{9(3x-1)}-\\frac{1}{9(3x-1)^{2}}+K\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068589 :question/id #uuid "3a2e21bb-c966-5429-98c0-9af209c45dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-510" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 1^2 - 4(1)(1) = -3 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{C}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+x+1)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=2,$ $B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} = \\dfrac{2x+1}{x^2+x+1} + \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{2}{x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+x+1.$ Then $(2x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068602 :question/id #uuid "ffb2b712-52b9-5a09-9ac4-10a88f72f57e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5193" :question/problem "![](images/76/76c0e8aed74f2dcf3a382f79768098eda4f359631cdffb812a0aeccbb9707976.png)\n\nFind the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above)." :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x-3 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068614 :question/id #uuid "8787186e-94bd-53da-9304-b0d3efbcb966" :question/math-academy-id "e-5823" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}\\right)+6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}\\right)+6a\\cdot a\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}}\\right)+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}\\right) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068615 :question/id #uuid "84d3492d-12bb-50c2-b5b4-5f08567eb4f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5825" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x}  = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6x-3}{2x}\\right) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{10x+7}{8x}\\right) & =8x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6x-3}{2x}\\right) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}\\right) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}\\right) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068621 :question/id #uuid "e2790549-46e6-51c2-b118-0c4a056f6d25" :question/math-academy-id "e-599" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x =e^{t}, \\, y= e^{2t}+1.$ Given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t,$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t,$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068622 :question/id #uuid "68d38626-1153-51ec-a3c6-d39c927fdd99" :question/math-academy-id "e-600" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx =  \\sin(2t), \\qquad y= 2\\cos (2t) \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt \\pi.\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -2 \\tan(2t),$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac\\pi 8.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y }{\\textrm d x^2},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} ( \\sin 2t ) = 2 \\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /8} & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-2{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068628 :question/id #uuid "0461f8ba-c3c5-5e22-a679-32ee4f2a01b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6149" :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = 1 - x^3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068629 :question/id #uuid "8e0fa2cd-2b29-510a-868f-4779de8536e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6260" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{x+2}{x} = \\dfrac{x-4}{x} + 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x,$ so the least common denominator is $x.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{x} & =\\frac{x-4}{x}+2 \\\\ x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+2}{x}\\right) & =x\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{x}+2) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{x} & =x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{x}+x\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{x}}+2x \\\\ x+2 & =x-4+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =x-4+2x \\\\ x+2 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3x-x & =2+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068630 :question/id #uuid "5d20e960-7554-5a05-b1e0-2b98b944e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-6261" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{1+x}{x} + \\dfrac{5+x}{6x} = \\dfrac{1-x}{3x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x,$ and $3x,$ so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x} & =\\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)+6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5+x}{6x}\\right) & =6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-x}{3x}\\right) \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)+6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5+x}{6x}\\right) & =2\\cdot 3x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-x}{3x}\\right) \\\\ 6\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1+x}{\\cancel{x}}\\right)+\\cancel{6x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5+x}{\\cancel{6x}}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{3x}}\\right) \\\\ 6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x) \\\\ 6+6x+5+x & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+11 & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+2x & =2-11 \\\\ 9x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068632 :question/id #uuid "e621f828-5fc0-55b6-b726-f9da59878574" :question/math-academy-id "e-6803" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=e^t, y=2e^{2t}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=4e^{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{4e^{2t}}{e^t} = 4e^t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068636 :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (t^2+1)e^{2t+1} \\, \\textrm{d} t.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068638 :question/id #uuid "a25b6d87-7779-5408-90d6-a558baaae70f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7055" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=2-y^2$ and the $y$-axis, as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/b7/b7d8a063bce77c53b67d672d1ee5a6f62873fd0e507f04e836625a38719039f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve with the $y$-axis by solving for $y$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nFinally, the volume can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{c}^{d}[g(y)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2 - y^{2})^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4y^{2} + y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4y^{2} + y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= 2π[4y - \\frac{4}{3}y^{3} + \\frac{1}{5}y^{5}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= 2π([4(\\sqrt{2}) - \\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt{2})^{3} + \\frac{1}{5}(\\sqrt{2})^{5}] - [0]) \\\\\n&= 2π(4\\sqrt{2} - \\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\\n&= 2π\\left(\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64π\\sqrt{2}}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since the region is symmetrical about the $x$-axis, in step $3$ above we were able to write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\pi\\int_{-\\sqrt 2}^\\sqrt 2 [g(y)]^2\\,\\textrm d y = 2\\pi\\int_{0}^\\sqrt 2 [g(y)]^2\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nThis step saved us a bit of work because the antiderivative evaluated to zero at the lower limit."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068644 :question/id #uuid "f415cae9-6b18-53c1-8c47-6e0a1f0de59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7313" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2} e^x\\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I = \\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin(2x)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I\\mathbin.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0))}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068659 :question/id #uuid "b8d7a1c6-b564-5eb1-b4ce-f15802db1b8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-89" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0} x^4\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\cos(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So we have\n\n$$\n-1 \\le \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 1\n$$\n\nand multiplying both sides by $x^4$ gives\n\n$$\n-x^4 \\le x^4\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le x^4\n$$\n\nNow, taking the limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-x^{4}) \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{4}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\le lim_(x → 0)(x^{4}) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{4}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich, by the squeeze theorem, implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0} x^4\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = 0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068665 :question/id #uuid "19c3a089-2a9d-5a4c-a5c2-55bf1b78d6e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-93" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0}x^3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac 3 x\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sin(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)}\\right|\\leq 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\mid x^3 \\mid$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3} & \\cdot & \\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) & \\le & x^{3}\\end{vmatrix};\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) & \\le & x^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nand using the definition of absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n-\\begin{vmatrix}x^3 & \\leq x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} \\leq & x^3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we're using absolute value here because $x^3$ has an odd power.\n\nNow, taking the limit as $x\\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) \\le lim_(x → 0)(\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the squeeze theorem, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0} x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} = 0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a078bc3-52b8-5683-9fc4-150ae7398d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247797} {:answer/id #uuid "8b19e982-7141-5e82-a18f-a52fb747bea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248666} {:answer/id #uuid "8efb2e07-a4b6-5537-b0a0-f09dfd5d70e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248694} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb592cd-386e-59b1-a348-58a407989329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249126} {:answer/id #uuid "e77da88f-a33c-559f-bb9e-505deb352791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfb592cd-386e-59b1-a348-58a407989329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249126} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d37130-cce0-5f71-97ce-44fc407f755f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "772ac28e-e690-51b7-a882-989bee35e089" :question/math-academy-id "q-114906" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4((2x-3)^{2}/4+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x-3)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-3}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28158dd4-39ca-5019-90a9-1412e1f4292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{27}(18(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260227} {:answer/id #uuid "2c034e3e-693d-50af-b988-d9aecc2ec8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260266} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6be99c-e198-5758-beb8-ea0815d772f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260399} {:answer/id #uuid "83ea00ef-d9d4-5586-9c07-bc1c3106b2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261021} {:answer/id #uuid "c965c4e1-f61a-5823-ac8b-7a49a2cdae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}(18(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ea00ef-d9d4-5586-9c07-bc1c3106b2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261021} :answer-field/id #uuid "64dfa2fa-b309-5c76-99ed-6aa7bf7c8336" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9435352d-2478-5c94-824b-ebf745d0d5a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-12706" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{9}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{2D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201f04d6-c002-5e0e-95fe-a7bfb6f3cd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266427} {:answer/id #uuid "4017a737-6567-579a-8f02-98712b431a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266723} {:answer/id #uuid "928bb7be-da0b-5934-b68c-38f79005663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267450} {:answer/id #uuid "932c94aa-6652-5204-910b-18fdb3861f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267461} {:answer/id #uuid "99faa052-1151-586c-8bf2-0746b9af0da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4017a737-6567-579a-8f02-98712b431a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266723} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6fc5f0-4245-5250-93b5-36ad6b67d400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e8b61bc-bb4e-5bb5-ab6c-973e00e2baa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-13647" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17591e19-47ea-5e53-8567-a48b8baf5772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272590} {:answer/id #uuid "394ca74c-477c-5700-a715-9afc902d952f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272888} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bc522a-40fe-5e91-8ac1-c3f775905d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273942} {:answer/id #uuid "e50697c6-d688-52f9-b1e9-a5c9ae3ed07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274410} {:answer/id #uuid "f86c0f26-519f-5a6f-a316-b1597568a6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394ca74c-477c-5700-a715-9afc902d952f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272888} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c33db9-aafd-5bcd-94ac-fa2dde73d85e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0920268-6184-5022-affb-309ac870c06c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14878" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f2bc2f-1fe4-514d-9417-86b74216500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272557} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd070c7-31c8-5ed5-9940-dea95036c4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273484} {:answer/id #uuid "a06b58e1-28a4-50ed-ac87-373471256b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273777} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e5e197-f359-54c9-a2da-6fab379f6f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274233} {:answer/id #uuid "dae5896a-987c-5514-adf7-468aaa639a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a06b58e1-28a4-50ed-ac87-373471256b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273777} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8a4da3-858c-5210-bec3-9666fbd56e66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8143064d-a592-5c44-b653-c218df46c5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-154678" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{x+1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\left(\\frac{x}{x+1}\\right)\\cdot {y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68e00ece-3332-5f0f-b59c-11045c060d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288767} {:answer/id #uuid "761a6701-8961-546a-b212-c98c4fbfc1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288850} {:answer/id #uuid "cd59d288-6229-56af-81b6-b4ead16a7499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289401} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7e3828-7766-562c-b628-34b107549138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289402} {:answer/id #uuid "f553b8da-e80a-55a1-a36f-a6e33554962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd59d288-6229-56af-81b6-b4ead16a7499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289401} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5829d08-72ba-56f4-86f2-a84b8f039183" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8f3f828-4a9b-54dd-a6d3-6f4468d7c192" :question/math-academy-id "q-238056" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-12x-35 & =-({x}^{2}+12x+35) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+[{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}]+35) \\\\  & =-((x+6)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-13/2$ | $-11/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c93a7f37-366d-55d9-b8e5-72e3403775bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066343}] :question/id #uuid "ef25c8d2-1dad-5a2d-bd5a-7116c326dda1" :question/math-academy-id "q-252615" :question/problem "If the equation $\\log_{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) =-3$ is equivalent to $b^{-3} = a$, then $b =$ {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9b2ceea-160c-57f9-bfc8-2b2562a94f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b492dd0b452525f4d034a582eca41805b6e4a5e1330eb196e1aba8d3c7030b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066359} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc59888-23f4-563f-a6a2-bfd3f03c3d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/13b44bf19e691008a4fd11235c5fdf6909d8993441eae5cc5e3af822c3e6919d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066360} {:answer/id #uuid "37b19d0b-89a1-59a8-a2e0-5e570b9be2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f2f20f1ea8e56a0a5eabe3428cee99a08fb145e905b85d565e29130c3cbe79a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066361} {:answer/id #uuid "6784ec7f-5f29-588c-96f0-11f1391d2974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c4adaa6e1263819fb272fec1addd2b702791317087c64b16ace99ad040fef91.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066362} {:answer/id #uuid "72c33791-0de6-5c9c-87a1-e14fa208bde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20cded3c8de6425592f7c2043e7e4ef46612da8e16d89e6155fd1858dc26b5e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066363}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4e18fac8-0878-523d-a162-c822f3cfa81b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066358}] :question/id #uuid "bb2cbc2f-6015-5b66-8cf7-43c1350445e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27449" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y = \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{x} + 4$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22293f66-8f3a-5695-afec-01f3179a56e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290779} {:answer/id #uuid "5804865b-c888-5992-9d88-e365a200c74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291005} {:answer/id #uuid "5a332add-8bf3-5cf0-bd5f-53c39d6c9fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291019} {:answer/id #uuid "82b46e6a-9584-5f81-9a0a-f2547e99ca3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291184} {:answer/id #uuid "baf5606e-afed-5f12-8e6a-d3ae8ec4be79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baf5606e-afed-5f12-8e6a-d3ae8ec4be79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291442} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82be02d-a62e-5425-8547-1fe3d1205f51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3dd2a7c3-0fdf-5e47-bc41-0d52827cdf03" :question/math-academy-id "q-27479" :question/problem "![](images/60/60a01bfb824bb2aae025aacd937726ac786b3c69842baf619de94570157df252.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fff885-9915-5843-bb72-8a7744b849f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/8122ba0ee07a532fd5475b871e5ee4ba8466df2a5a72772014cf78e7dcb8401b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290843} {:answer/id #uuid "50eeae70-6c08-5520-9594-ea947132f442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6326564f0ad0359302d9281204d49252c8f855e2bd4b3f015c05d4e3ab455e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290979} {:answer/id #uuid "70889ca0-310e-511d-a33e-65301e438d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5d3b33a66e403c77ac3bc705122d689804ee3f0267d282dc2d20c82df126d98b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291114} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4fd38e-1e33-54fb-abb4-4b0638f39eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/178254379bc5d68d11aa978dd39588c45b705a78b113b9522905cb2170cc92aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291316} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b3888b-7063-5ca9-97d5-85099061807a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/6622a8663091f5a4b41fa142b20c78e5f129cffee9fc1c741e19de8458397ecd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70889ca0-310e-511d-a33e-65301e438d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5d3b33a66e403c77ac3bc705122d689804ee3f0267d282dc2d20c82df126d98b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291114} :answer-field/id #uuid "709ba56b-4ede-5241-a344-eb46109faca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f06451e-1e75-5602-aba0-98e72047ec47" :question/math-academy-id "q-27480" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1e/1e7f53108e1bbfee39ed331ea4b0d7954b8bdf98310ebe2376008835b908bbbe.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/99/99d164fc056b13447a549abffd210bb4338e36ec6fe67d148daa5bb68a9a07a5.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "530aeef4-0e1c-5b47-b9e7-1248f272f282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290986} {:answer/id #uuid "742f0baf-8f00-5d80-9a69-0d7e3c19c536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291129} {:answer/id #uuid "b8714c5f-ec58-59b5-a14b-cb65ccc08d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291432} {:answer/id #uuid "c6221e46-bb2f-5119-9ff5-b46dd1ba8e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291486} {:answer/id #uuid "f14ef768-3a64-5012-a1cb-d243e41059f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8714c5f-ec58-59b5-a14b-cb65ccc08d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291432} :answer-field/id #uuid "68740722-6b45-5b07-9787-dbe68c200ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29d59c32-2de3-52b4-a443-c1163844c843" :question/math-academy-id "q-27544" :question/problem "![](images/76/768d0be7926a8f66d7b2ac193e842f7567d40e9c2217376b93ed284154520539.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ hence $c=4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+4 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{0}+4 & =6 \\\\ k+4 & =6 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=2-4=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a61edb-a827-5360-a4a4-72430cd2463f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18<x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506861} {:answer/id #uuid "550b441b-3efa-5e9e-9e1f-f7f49ccb951f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506876} {:answer/id #uuid "d947d0e6-9a40-5168-996a-f3ee438e94b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507146} {:answer/id #uuid "da7ea81a-6c3e-5d46-99ca-109614820347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507150} {:answer/id #uuid "e900a3b8-d179-5b41-b311-f02d66693bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15<x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7ea81a-6c3e-5d46-99ca-109614820347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507150} :answer-field/id #uuid "5696f866-cced-54cd-962d-9825c54cd08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b61c5dfe-34c0-5830-8c59-cd20d813f4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-3070" :question/problem "What is the solution to $-10<\\frac{6-2x}{3}<0?$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & <\\frac{6-2x}{3}<0 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-10) & <3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6-2x}{3}\\right)<3\\cdot 0 \\\\ -30 & <6-2x<0 \\\\ -30-6 & <6-2x-6<0-6 \\\\ -36 & <-2x<-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-2.$ However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36 & <-2x<-6 \\\\ \\frac{-36}{-2} & >\\frac{-2x}{-2}>\\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\ 18 & >x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $18>x>3,$ but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n3<x<18\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c74308bb-5312-5958-b720-065684db080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066553} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} {:answer/id #uuid "28a7f235-57a0-53cc-a737-590d73e17205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066556} {:answer/id #uuid "23dbda11-5ccf-5052-90ff-4a04744a31b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503050} {:answer/id #uuid "d40037db-e219-58c5-9dc7-f0a266a679e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} :answer-field/id #uuid "46437d48-53c7-532c-8f90-902783817395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae074857-e82d-57ff-b966-0c2516135214" :question/math-academy-id "q-3094" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}$ given that $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}} &= \\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2x}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\ &= |\\frac{2x}{5}| \\\\ &= \\frac{|2x|}{|5|} \\\\ &= \\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5} &= \\frac{2\\cdot x}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fc67dc-b6d4-5aec-9f3f-e536248f2662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295556} {:answer/id #uuid "81da5bb2-d80a-5b1f-8f32-a080a945e8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295966} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8b2325-1e23-5ed2-9d75-d45fcc13dc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296180} {:answer/id #uuid "d16d8b22-5555-50c0-8fbe-c408fc0043ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296370} {:answer/id #uuid "e5039308-86b2-5cc3-9e65-3b16d8afb2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32fc67dc-b6d4-5aec-9f3f-e536248f2662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295556} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ed423-335e-5ce4-a744-bcd4082bb017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0772e5dc-a19a-5346-a868-136cb8608ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-31364" :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ebbe2d-74ec-5380-9a07-702d026d313d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73590d55299fc519582252723f34bfa143818da7f03a16f7aeb8d208217ee3d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066589} {:answer/id #uuid "00accd83-f7f9-57bb-aa6e-78f38f349bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b53396e5736d8e09a11acef07594950ee50a0c027e190f0c8eb85880cf721494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066590} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7e45dc-8e5a-5e38-a11e-07a10f544e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066591} {:answer/id #uuid "319a2339-b438-5f58-b459-e90c8e2d9866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a84109a9d68fe4b185ede5ab47c8ea2c07acd609367b629b594ab0b8e415bc82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066592} {:answer/id #uuid "7941c9a0-4881-5921-a1a9-38e914122b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e457f65868b58c9246d17744317ad6f76a96c8bd3b024f4fe2d37523a6490fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066593}] :answer-field/id #uuid "375a8c1c-ecfc-5e8e-99de-762874c0a19a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066588} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f871c9fe-ce5c-57e0-b3ab-1c1c8b065a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b53396e5736d8e09a11acef07594950ee50a0c027e190f0c8eb85880cf721494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390607} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfaa41c-4be2-5d25-a838-46703edf0110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a84109a9d68fe4b185ede5ab47c8ea2c07acd609367b629b594ab0b8e415bc82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390608} {:answer/id #uuid "857c287c-8899-5fbc-b5f9-5a9ea6523a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e457f65868b58c9246d17744317ad6f76a96c8bd3b024f4fe2d37523a6490fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390609} {:answer/id #uuid "b422f31c-7ac6-593f-9a66-3623920a036a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73590d55299fc519582252723f34bfa143818da7f03a16f7aeb8d208217ee3d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390610} {:answer/id #uuid "e921280b-88b6-51bc-a473-f6af4ac53ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e921280b-88b6-51bc-a473-f6af4ac53ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390611} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6f078f-4b80-5f34-98d4-3100f6b4d2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3af553bc-4342-536e-bf61-1a65d8389d56" :question/math-academy-id "q-31424" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/41/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1542b9a1-bbaf-5904-a5c7-b5919ecda82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295389} {:answer/id #uuid "25c47fe3-e0fb-55cf-b576-2f60089246b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295475} {:answer/id #uuid "cc00f0e9-2d17-5f1d-8d09-9ad01edcf4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296336} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8680f1-d9a7-5544-9ace-5e08cf854456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296525} {:answer/id #uuid "ee39b011-4878-5671-a4b1-c69369c36bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed8680f1-d9a7-5544-9ace-5e08cf854456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296525} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e31b49-fb11-5ce3-b695-35277ae46aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05d2d2b4-376a-594e-a55c-c9c3393ae7ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-31596" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
